  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has 40 fields, including `timestamp`, `response`, `response.keyword`, `clientip`, `bytes`, `url`, `geo.dest`, and `hour_of_day`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-017a0df3 Web logs' (id `s4-017a0df3-web-logs-01de612393d1`) has two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count by `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of count by `response` terms, top 10. I used `response` rather than `response.keyword`, since that is the field name the data view lists first.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-017a0df3 5xx errors' (id `9001a44c-9daf-4401-bbf3-cc8754f49be8`) is a `.es-query` rule on `stackAlerts`.\n   - It runs every 1m and looks at a 5-minute window on `timestamp`.\n   - It fires when the count of documents with `response` between `\"500\"` and `\"599\"` is greater than 10.\n   - It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n   - The 5xx filter is a lexicographic range on the `response` field. That works for three-digit codes, but it is my choice, not something you specified.\n\n4. **Panel edit:** The response-code panel (index 1) is now titled \"Responses by code\". I re-read the dashboard and the new title is there. The rule read-back shows `enabled: true`, status `pending`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
